      "span_text": "Session Summary - Objective: Create an alphabetized, comma-separated list of vegetables from the provided items using botanical criteria, ensuring no botanical fruits are included. Provide transparent, evidence-based reasoning and preserve uncertainties/alternative interpretations. - Approach: 1) Established authoritative botanical definitions for “fruit” and a practical working convention for “vegetable” as edible non-fruit plant parts (roots, stems, leaves, flower buds). 2) Conducted a single targeted web-research subtask to compile item-by-item classifications with reputable citations (primarily Britannica, with Kew’s Plants of the World Online for Capsicum). 3) Identified edge cases (notably basil as an herb leaf) and produced two candidate vegetable lists: a strict botanical list (includes edible leaves like basil) and a conservative list (excludes basil). 4) Selected the conservative list as the final answer to satisfy a “stickler” requirement and avoid any category ambiguity. Methodology and Research Actions - One focused external research subtask was executed to gather authoritative definitions and item classifications, returning a structured report with quotes and links. - Sources prioritized: Encyclopaedia Britannica (definitions and plant entries) and Kew’s Plants of the World Online (Capsicum annuum fruit type). Definitions and Criteria (authoritative sources) - Botanical fruit: • “Botanically, a fruit is a mature ovary and its associated parts. It usually contains seeds, which have developed from the enclosed ovule after fertilization.” (Britannica – Fruit: https://www.britannica.com/science/fruit-plant-reproductive-body) • “After fertilization, the ovule develops into a seed, and the ovary develops into a fruit.” (Britannica – Plant reproductive system/Angiosperms: https://www.britannica.com/science/plant-reproductive-system/Angiosperms) - Vegetable (working convention in this task): A culinary/market term rather than botanical; tre