/plant/celery) - zucchini — Botanical fruit (pepo; a type of berry in cucurbits). • “Pepo, a berry with a hard rind… typical of the gourd family.” (Britannica – Pepo: https://www.britannica.com/science/pepo) • Zucchini is a summer squash (Cucurbita pepo) (Britannica – Zucchini: https://www.britannica.com/plant/zucchini) - lettuce — Vegetable (leaves; edible non-fruit plant parts). • “Lettuce… leafy annual… leaves eaten fresh.” (Britannica: https://www.britannica.com/plant/lettuce) - peanuts — Seeds from a legume fruit (pod); consumed portion is the seed (not a vegetable). • “Peanut… edible seed of the leguminous plant Arachis hypogaea.” (Britannica: https://www.britannica.com/plant/peanut) Ambiguities and Edge Cases - Vegetable is not a botanical category; it is culinary/market. For this task, vegetables were treated as edible non-fruit plant parts to ensure no botanical fruits appear on the vegetable list. - Basil: Edible leaves make it a non-fruit plant part, so including it as a “vegetable” under the working convention is defensible. However, because many cooks categorize basil separately as an herb, the conservative list excludes basil to avoid ambiguity for a stickler. Candidate Vegetable Lists (alphabetized; exact spellings) - Strict botanical vegetable list (includes edible leaves, including basil): broccoli, celery, fresh basil, lettuce, sweet potatoes - Conservative list (excludes herb-like basil; safest for a stickler): broccoli, celery, lettuce, sweet potatoes Confirmation of Exclusions - Botanical fruits excluded: green beans (legume pod), corn and rice (caryopses/grains), bell pepper (berry), zucchini (pepo), plums (drupe), whole allspice (berries), acorns (nut), peanuts (seeds from legume fruit). Non-plant/processed items excluded: milk, eggs, flour, Oreos. Seeds extracted from fruits (coffee beans, peanuts) are not vegetables under the adopted criteria. FINAL ANSWER broccoli, celery, lettuce, sweet potatoes Useful Facts and Quotes (directly relevant) 